---
title: Reading a home timeline — the hybrid merge across two caches
---
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor U as User (browser)
    participant G as gateway
    participant TL as timeline-service
    participant RM as redis-main
    participant RC as redis-celeb
    participant D as DynamoDB
    participant T as tweet-service

    U->>G: GET /api/v1/timeline?limit=50
    G->>G: validate JWT, check rate limit
    G->>TL: forward (subject = userId)

    par materialised portion
        TL->>RM: GET tl:{userId}:p0
        alt cache hit
            RM-->>TL: tweet IDs from non-celebrity authors
        else cache miss
            TL->>D: Query timelines, userId = :u,<br/>ScanIndexForward=false, Limit 50
            D-->>TL: tweet IDs
            TL->>RM: SET tl:{userId}:p0, TTL 60s
        end
    and celebrity portion
        TL->>RC: SMEMBERS celebrities
        TL->>D: Query follows where followerId = :u
        TL->>TL: intersect to a small celebrity set
        TL->>RC: LRANGE celeb:tweets:{c} 0 49 for each c
        alt cache miss on a celebrity
            TL->>D: Query tweets.gsi_author,<br/>authorId = :c, Limit 50
            Note right of TL: single-flight lock — N concurrent<br/>misses cause one DynamoDB read,<br/>protecting a hot partition key
        end
    end

    TL->>TL: merge both lists by UUIDv7 order, take 50
    TL->>T: GET /internal/tweets?ids=...  (hydrate)
    T-->>TL: tweet bodies, media keys, counters
    TL-->>G: timeline page
    G-->>U: 200 OK

    Note over TL,D: Losing either cache costs latency and DynamoDB spend,<br/>never data — the timelines table is durable.<br/>Uncached, this path would cost roughly $1,000/day at target load,<br/>which is why cache hit rate is a budget metric, not just a latency one.
